4. FINDINGS
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING 1 — BLOCKING. A CLASS, 5 instances, all in packages/commands/src/probe.ts.
A record write for a pair that spent NO TURN destroys that pair's own
due-trigger evidence: `harnessVersion` is dropped everywhere, and `at` is
overwritten with the run's clock in 4 of the 5 paths.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This reintroduces, in the writer, the stranding class Sol's closure-pass carve
finding 4 raised — the finding the brief itself calls "the one finding that is
lost work, not wasted spend" and for which revision 4 moved the version onto the
probe. The brief's own invariant:

  "a skipped pair keeps its OLD harnessVersion, so it stays version-due next
   tick and the tail drains — exactly the rotation the ordering promises"
                                     (*Why the version lives on the probe*)

It does not keep it.

EVERY INSTANCE (I read all of probe.ts and enumerated every upsert that writes a
row without a turn; `git grep -n "upsert("` in probe.ts returns 6 call sites, 5
of which are no-turn writes):

  i.   probe.ts:345  step 2, mark-before-spend
         upsert(c.id, c.harness, { harness, state:"unverified",
                                   at: prior?.at ?? nowIso, note:"probe due" })
       Preserves prior `at` (correct) but DROPS prior `harnessVersion`, and
       synthesizes `at: nowIso` for a pair that was never probed — which
       destroys the never-probed classification (see FINDING 3).
  ii.  probe.ts:420  condemnHarness (control failed)
         at: nowIso — OVERWRITES the prior `at`, drops `harnessVersion`. Zero turns.
  iii. probe.ts:435  the no-control branch
         at: nowIso — same, for every candidate on a control-less harness. Zero turns.
  iv.  probe.ts:360  unknown harness
         at: nowIso — same. Zero turns.
  v.   probe.ts:370  harness available() false ("harness unavailable")
         at: nowIso — same. Zero turns.

PROOF A — hermetic, reproducible. Script: $TMPDIR/censor/censor-probe.mts.
A record seeded with two claude-code pairs, each carrying
harnessVersion "2.1.270 (Claude Code)"; the control hits a usage limit so the
tail is condemned with zero turns:
  turns: 1   skipped: [{"id":"zzz-tail","harness":"claude-code"}]
  prior harnessVersion on the skipped tail: "2.1.270 (Claude Code)"
  after:  {"harness":"claude-code","state":"unverified",
           "at":"2026-09-25T12:00:05.000Z","note":"control failed: claude-code"}
  RESULT: DROPPED (harnessVersion lost); `at` also advanced 12:00:00 -> 12:00:05
  top-level snapshot: preserved (that half is correct)

PROOF B — live, on the real battery record (§3.8(b)'s SIGINT).
Four codex pairs carried harnessVersion "codex-cli 0.153.4" before the run and
spent no turn during it:
  BEFORE                                      AFTER (zero turns spent on each)
  gpt-5.5       'codex-cli 0.153.4'    ->     None   note 'probe due'
  gpt-5.6-luna  'codex-cli 0.153.4'    ->     None   note 'probe due'
  gpt-5.6-terra 'codex-cli 0.153.4'    ->     None   note 'probe due'
  gpt-6-astra   'codex-cli 0.153.4'    ->     None   note 'probe due'
They are now indistinguishable from "never probed". W-071's trigger fires on
defined-vs-defined only, so it can never fire for them again.

PROOF C — live, the `at` overwrite (instance ii), one free claude turn:
  BEFORE  claude-sonnet-5  at 2026-09-24T22:15:25.078Z  "control failed: claude-code"
  run     `probe --model claude-sonnet-5 --harness claude-code` -> 1 turn spent
          (the control; claude-sonnet-5 itself spent ZERO)
  AFTER   claude-sonnet-5  at 2026-09-24T22:17:29.682Z  "control failed: claude-code"
A pair that has never once been probed now carries a two-minutes-ago evidence
timestamp. Repeat the run and its `at` tracks "now" forever while it is never
probed — and `at` is what W-071's age trigger reads. A harness with a broken
control stamps its entire candidate set fresh on every run, so when the control
heals, nothing on that harness is age-due either.

WHY THE SUITE DID NOT CATCH IT — a spec-mandated assertion is missing.
Behaviour 9(iii) is specified as:
  "the resulting ModelsRecord preserves BOTH the prior probes[].harnessVersion
   on unprobed rows AND the prior top-level snapshot value — a writer that
   overwrites EITHER with undefined fails."
probe.test.ts implements only the second half (one check: "(iii) the top-level
snapshot is preserved when the live lookup fails"). Its seeded row carries no
harnessVersion at all and IS probed, so the per-pair half is asserted nowhere.
Reading "unprobed rows" as "rows that are not candidates" would make the
assertion vacuous — already covered by behaviour 10(a)'s verbatim retention —
and vacuous assertions are what Sol finding 9 closed.

Two candidate roots, both in scope for one fix; the choice is the builder's:
  (a) carry the prior probe's `at` AND `harnessVersion` through every no-turn
      write, only a real turn updating either; and/or
  (b) stamp each turn with its own clock reading rather than one `nowIso` per
      battery — the brief's own seam example shows three distinct per-probe
      times inside one battery ("12:00:01", "12:00:04", "12:00:06"), which
      also fixes FINDING 3 for free.
Whichever is taken, behaviour 9(iii)'s missing per-pair case must land with it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING 2 — BLOCKING. Behaviour 10(c) does not discriminate. The exact
assertion Sol's closure pass REOPENED on discrimination grounds is still
non-discriminating, for a second reason.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The brief: "A classifier that treats corrupt as fresh sorts it last and fails;
one that lets NaN fall through a comparator sorts it unpredictably and fails."
The second clause does not hold.

The shipped implementation is CORRECT (probe.ts:297 maps an unparseable `at` to
-Infinity). The TEST is what fails to bite: probe.test.ts's `only` array for
10(c) is already in the asserted order —
  [aaa-ctrl, bbb-corrupt, ccc-old, ddd-recent]
— and the assertion is that the start calls arrive in exactly that order. With
NaN reaching the comparator, ECMA-262 SortCompare coerces a NaN result to +0, so
the corrupt pair compares "equal" to everything and V8's stable sort leaves it
where the input put it: position 2. The assertion passes.

MUTATION M2, `return Number.isNaN(ms) ? -Infinity : ms;` -> `return ms;`:
  node --import tsx packages/commands/src/probe.test.ts . 10   exit 0   NO BITE

PROOF — the same three seeded pairs, the only change being the order the pairs
are presented in (script: $TMPDIR/censor/b10c-discrim.mts):

  SHIPPED code (-Infinity):
    input == asserted order   order=aaa-ctrl,bbb-corrupt,ccc-old,ddd-recent  matches=true
    shuffled input            order=aaa-ctrl,bbb-corrupt,ccc-old,ddd-recent  matches=true
  MUTATED code (NaN falls through):
    input == asserted order   order=aaa-ctrl,bbb-corrupt,ccc-old,ddd-recent  matches=true   <- passes
    shuffled input            order=aaa-ctrl,ccc-old,ddd-recent,bbb-corrupt  matches=false  <- bites

Reordering the `only` array by one line makes the assertion bite and M2 fail.
The correct implementation is order-independent, which is the property worth
asserting. Behaviour 4's order assertion was checked for the same weakness and
is sound — its input order differs from the asserted order, so it discriminates.
10(c) is the only instance.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING 3 — BLOCKING (same class as FINDING 1, instance i). The rotation the
brief promises — "whatever a stopped run skipped has the oldest evidence next
run, so it heads the queue" — fails on a virgin record, which is the real first
battery. A pair can be starved indefinitely.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 2 stamps a never-probed pair with `at: nowIso`, the SAME value every pair
probed in that run receives (one `nowIso` per battery). The skipped pair is then
not older than the pairs that completed, so next run's tiebreak falls to
(id, harness) — a deterministic order plus a stop, which is exactly the
starvation Sol finding 4's second half named.

PROOF — hermetic, no pre-seeded record, a usage limit on the 2nd turn every run
(script: $TMPDIR/censor/censor-probe.mts, section B):
  run1 order: ["aaa-ctrl","bbb-limit"]   skipped: ["zzz-tail"]
  run1 record: aaa-ctrl at=12:00:00  bbb-limit at=12:00:00 'usage limit'
               zzz-tail at=12:00:00 'probe due'      <- same instant as the probed pairs
  run2 order: ["aaa-ctrl","bbb-limit"]   <- zzz-tail skipped AGAIN
  run3 order: ["aaa-ctrl","zzz-tail","bbb-limit"]
  zzz-tail probed in run2? False   in run3? True

Behaviour 4 asserts this very property and passes only because its test primes
three distinct prior `at` values through three single-candidate batteries first
(probe.test.ts:348-359, "Prime distinct `at` values in the desired relative age
order"). With prior evidence the property holds; with none it does not, and a
first battery has none. The behaviour needs a virgin-record case.

Not permanent starvation in every configuration (it drained on run 3 above, at
the cost of a wasted turn), but it is not the promised rotation, and with a
recurring stop at a fixed position it does not drain at all.

================================================================================
5. RULINGS ON THE FIVE DISCLOSED DEVIATIONS
================================================================================

(1) ProbeBatteryOptions.fs — ACCEPTED as a necessary seam, with one note.
    The empirical claim is correct: node:fs's named ESM exports are not
    writable from outside the module, so behaviour 6(b) — "the stub throws
    AFTER the temp file exists and BEFORE the rename" — is not hermetically
    testable any other way. It is the same shape as the three seams the brief
    already publishes (harnesses / listModels / versions) and the brief itself
    asks in *For Sol* 5 whether that guard is implementable hermetically; this
    is the answer. Mutation M1 confirms the guard bites. NOT interface creep.
    Note, non-blocking: the seam declares `existsSync` and nothing uses it —
    persist() never calls it and readModelsRecord uses the real one. Dead
    member; drop it.

(2) runProbe(args, opts = {}) — ACCEPTED, and the near-miss handled correctly.
    It matches runTalk/runTick verbatim and is a defaulted second parameter, so
    `runProbe(args)` behaves exactly as the published signature says.
    On the near-miss: the builder's own tests silently spent a real claude turn
    and called real `codex debug models`, caught only because a sandbox
    network-violation log surfaced it. The fix is verified — my own sentinel run
    (§1) shows zero vendor invocations across the whole suite with both binaries
    shadowed. Credit for disclosing it.
    RULING ON THE CLASS: this needs a STANDING GUARD, and it is not this
    round's fix. Nothing in the repo currently prevents the next verb's tests
    from spending real money; the only thing that caught it was a sandbox log
    nobody is obliged to read, and the injection discipline it was fixed with
    is a memory, not a rule — which CLAUDE.md's own standing rule forbids
    ("Process enforcement is a check rule or a hook, never a memory or prose").
    The guard should be test-harness-level and mechanical: a sentinel PATH
    shadowing `claude`/`codex` for the duration of `npm test`, failing the run
    if either is invoked. My §1 run is a working prototype of exactly that in
    ~6 lines of shell. FILED FOR THE RETRO as its own check-rule or test-infra
    opus; a fix attempted inside W-069 would be scope creep.

(3) @bisellium/server devDep in packages/commands — ACCEPTED.
    Test-only, and the reverse precedent already exists (apps/server declares
    @bisellium/commands as a devDependency). Behaviour 9 is REQUIRED by the
    brief to assert through the public `new Store({…}).api.officina().models`
    precisely so store.ts stays untouched (Sol finding 8), so the dependency is
    the brief's own consequence. No runtime dependency is added and no cycle
    exists at runtime. package.json is an undeclared file in the diff, but it is
    the mechanical consequence of a declared requirement, disclosed.

(4) The duplicated `row.harness ?? "claude-code"` — ACCEPTED as unavoidable,
    BUT IT NEEDS A DRIFT GUARD AND HAS NONE. Non-blocking on its own; fold it
    into the round-2 fix.
    The impossibility claim checks out: packages/cli already depends on
    packages/commands, so the import direction is a real cycle, and
    packages/commands/tsconfig.json's rootDir:"src" makes a relative
    cross-package import a typecheck failure. The comment says "unlikely to
    drift", which is a hope, not a guard.
    There is NO test pinning the two definitions equal — I searched: no test
    imports both `harnessForSella` (packages/cli/src/tick.ts) and
    `defaultHarness` (probe.ts, which is module-private and unexported), and no
    test asserts any shared constant. Two silently divergable definitions of
    which harness an unqualified seat runs on. If they diverge, `probe` probes
    one harness and `talk` uses another, and the record is confidently wrong
    about a pair that was never tested.
    The lazy guard: hoist the literal `"claude-code"` default into
    @bisellium/shim (which both packages already depend on) and have both read
    it — one line each, no new file, no cycle, and the duplication disappears
    rather than being policed. Failing that, a test that asserts the two
    resolvers agree on a harness-less row.

(5) The third collision-fixture seat — IN SCOPE, and in fact REQUIRED.
    Behaviour 9 mandates that "against the collision fixture, a battery whose
    probes for one id disagree across harnesses prints a warning line". The
    control rule makes that impossible without a codex-seated candidate: with no
    codex seat, codex has no control, every codex row is "no control" with zero
    turns, and no disagreement can arise. `codex-lead / gpt-5.6-sol / harness:
    codex` is the minimum that makes the brief's own behaviour 9 reachable. The
    fixture is a declared file; no scope creep.

================================================================================
6. OBSERVATIONS — non-blocking, recorded, no action required this round
================================================================================
a. Every probe in one battery shares the battery's `nowIso`, but HarnessProbe.at
   is specified as "when this pair's turn ran" and the brief's seam example
   shows three distinct times inside one battery. Immaterial to a day-scale
   staleness threshold, but it is the proximate cause of FINDING 3 and fixing
   it fixes both.
b. The disagreement warning iterates every entry in the record, including
   entries this battery did not probe, so a stale disagreement from an earlier
   run re-prints. The brief says "a battery whose probes for one id disagree".
   Cosmetic.
c. A targeted `--model X --harness H` run spends the control's turn WITHOUT
   marking the control first — the control is not in `due`, so step 2 skips it.
   The window is one turn wide and the control's own row is rewritten
   immediately after, so nothing false is durable; noting it because the brief
   says the control is "added to the run".
d. `bisellium probe` discards `total_cost_usd` from the claude envelope. No
   behaviour requires recording it, but the brief's own acceptance asks the
   censor for "the claude-side cost from the envelopes", which the shipped verb
   cannot supply — I had to shim the binary boundary to read it. Worth a field
   or a printed line in some later opus.
e. codexListModels correctly does NOT pass harnessEnv, faithful to "a rewrite is
   not a move"; harnessVersions correctly does. Both as specified.
f. The hoist is exactly the three bounded edits; `async` dropped from the moved
   function's signature is semantically identical for a Promise-returning
   function. apps/server's suite passes unchanged — the evidence for the move.

